= 5x + 0 \\\\\n10 &= 5x\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSecond, we apply the multiplication principle, dividing both sides by $5$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n10 &= 5x \\\\\n\\frac{10}{5} &= \\frac{5x}{5} \\\\\n2 &= \\frac{5x}{5} \\\\\n2 &= x\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $2=x$, we conclude that the solution is $x=2$." 13194139535252 17592186045417 false] [17592186054385 1074 "To save time, we will keep the variable on the right-hand side as we solve. First, we apply the addition principle, adding $8$ to both sides:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2 & =5x-8 \\\\ 2+8 & =5x-8+8 \\\\ 10 & =5x+0 \\\\ 10 & =5x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSecond, we apply the multiplication principle, dividing both sides by $5\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}10 & =5x \\\\ \\frac{10}{5} & =\\frac{5x}{5} \\\\ 2 & =\\frac{\\cancel{5}x}{\\cancel{5}} \\\\ 2 & =x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $2=x,$ we conclude that the solution is $x=2.$" 13194139535252 17592186045417 true] [17592186054386 1071 "Solve the equation $7-\\dfrac{p}{3} =9$." 13194139535252 17592186045417 false] [17592186054386 1071 "Solve the equation $7-\\dfrac{p}{3} =9.$" 13194139535252 17592186045417 true] [17592186054386 1074 "After converting the equation to a more familiar form, we apply the addition principle, subtracting $7$ from both sides:\n\n$$\n\\require{cancel}\n\t\\eqalign{\n               7-\\dfrac{p}{3} &=9 \\\n    \t\t-\\dfrac{p}{3}+7 &=9\\\n\t\t-\\dfrac{p}{3}+7-7 &= 9-7 \\\n\t\t-\\dfrac{p}{3}+0 &= 2 \\\n\t\t-\\dfrac{p}{3} &=2\n\t}\n$$\n\nThen, we apply the multiplication principle. We multiply by $-1$ to get rid of the negative sign, and again by $3$ to get rid of the fraction:\n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\n               -\\dfrac{p}{3} &=2 \\\n              (-1) \\cdot \\left( -\\dfrac{p}{3} \\right) &=(-1) \\cdot 2 \\\n\t\t\\dfrac{p}{3} &=-2 \\\n\t\t\\cancel{3} \\cdot \\dfrac{p}{\\cancel{3}} &=3 \\cdot (-2) \\   \n\t\tp &=-6\n\t}\n$$" 13194139535252 17592186045417 true] [17592186054386 1074 "After converting the equation to a more familiar form, we apply the addition principle, subtracting $7$ from both sides:\n\n$$\n\\require{cancel} \\begin{aligned} 7-\\dfrac{p}{3} &=9 \\\\ -\\dfrac{p}{3}+7 &=9 \\\\ -\\dfrac{p}{3}+7-7 &= 9-7 \\\\ -\\dfrac{p}{3}+0 &= 2 \\\\ -\\dfrac{p}{3} &=2 \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we apply the multiplication principle. We multiply by $-1$ to get rid of the negative sign, and again by $3$ to get rid of the fraction:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} -\\dfrac{p}{3} &=2 \\\\ (-1) \\cdot \\left(-\\dfrac{p}{3} \\right) &=(-1) \\cdot 2 \\ \\dfrac{p}{3} &=-2 \\ \\cancel{3} \\cdot \\dfrac{p}{\\cancel{3}} &=3 \\cdot (-2) \\\\ p &=-6 \\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535252 17592186045417 false] [17592186054387 1071 "Solve the equation $-\\dfrac{z}{3}-\\dfrac 1 2 =\\dfrac 7 2$." 13194139535252 17592186045417 false] [17592186054387 1071 "Solve the equation $-\\dfrac{z}{3}-\\dfrac 1 2 =\\dfrac 7 2.$" 13194139535252 17592186045417 true] [17592186054387 1074 "First, we apply the addition principle, adding\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{2}\n$$\n\nto both sides:\n\n$$\n\\require{cancel} \\begin{aligned} -\\dfrac{z}{3}-\\dfrac 1 2 &=\\dfrac 7 2 \\\\[5pt] -\\dfrac{z}{3} - \\dfrac 1 2+\\dfrac 1 2 &= \\dfrac 7 2+\\dfrac 1 2 \\\\[5pt] -\\dfrac{z}{3} + 0 &= \\dfrac 7 2+\\dfrac 1 2 \\\\[5pt] -\\dfrac{z}{3} &=\\dfrac 8 2 \\\\[5pt] -\\dfrac{z}{3} &=4 \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we apply the multiplication principle. We multiply by $-1$ to get rid of the negative sign, and again by $3$ to get rid of the fraction.\n\n$$\n\\require{cancel} \\begin{aligned} -\\dfrac{z}{3} &=4 \\\\ (-1) \\cdot \\left(-\\dfrac{z}{3} \\right) &= (-1) \\cdot 4 \\\\[5pt] \\dfrac{z}{3} &=-4 \\\\[5pt] \\cancel{3} \\cdot \\dfrac{z}{\\cancel{3}} &= 3 \\cdot (-4) \\\\[5pt] z &=-12 \\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535252 17592186045417 false] [17592186054387 1074 "First, we apply the addition principle, adding $\\dfrac{1}{2}$ to both sides:\n\n$$\n\\require{cancel}\n\t\\eqalign{\n    \t\t-\\dfrac{z}{3}-\\dfrac 1 2 &=\\dfrac 7 2\\[5pt]\n\t\t-\\dfrac{z}{3} - \\dfrac 1 2+\\dfrac 1 2 &= \\dfrac 7 2+\\dfrac 1 2 \\[5pt]\n-\\dfrac{z}{3} + 0 &= \\dfrac 7 2+\\dfrac 1 2 \\[5pt]\n\t\t-\\dfrac{z}{3} &=\\dfrac 8 2 \\[5pt]\n\t\t-\\dfrac{z}{3} &=4\n\t}\n$$\n\nThen, we apply the multiplication principle. We multiply by $-1$ to get rid of the negative sign, and again by $3$ to get rid of the fraction.\n\n$$\n\\require{cancel}\n\t\\eqalign{\n-\\dfrac{z}{3} &=4 \\\n(-1) \\cdot \\left( -\\dfrac{z}{3} \\right) &= (-1) \\cdot 4 \\[5pt]\n\t\t\\dfrac{z}{3} &=-4 \\[5pt]\n\t\t\\cancel{3} \\cdot \\dfrac{z}{\\cancel{3}} &= 3 \\cdot (-4) \\[5pt]   \n\t\tz &=-12\n\t}\n$$" 13194139535252 17592186045417 true] [17592186054411 1071 "Find the solution to the equation $16 + x =10$." 13194139535252 17592186045417 false] [17592186054411 1071 "Find the solution to the equation $16 + x =10.$" 13194139535252 17592186045417 true] [17592186054411 1074 "First, let's rewrite the left-hand-side so that it's similar to what we've seen before.\n\n$$\nx + 16 = 10\n$$\n\nTo isolate $x$, we need to perform the *opposite* of adding $16$ to $x$, which is *subtracting* $16$ from $x$. Remember to perform the same operation on the right-hand side.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} x + 16 &= 10 \\\\ x + 16 - 16 &= 10 - 16 \\\\ x + 0 &= -6 \\\\ x &= -6 \\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535252 17592186045417 false] [17592186054411 1074 "First, let's rewrite the left-hand-side so that it's similar to what we've seen before.\n\n$$\nx + 16 = 10\n$$\n\nTo isolate $x$, we need to perform the *opposite* of adding $16$ to $x$, which is *subtracting* $16$ from $x.$ Remember to perform the same operation on the right-hand side.\n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\n    \t\tx + 16 &= 10 \\\n    \t\tx + 16 - 16 &= 10 - 16 \\\n\t\tx + 0 &= -6 \\\n\t\tx &= -6\n\t}\n$$" 13194139535252 17592186045417 true] [17592186054412 1071 "Solve the equation $x-8 =6$." 13194139535252 17592186045417 false] [17592186054412 1071 "Solve the equation $x-8 =6.$" 13194139535252 17592186045417 true] [17592186054412 1074 "To isolate $x$, we need to perform the *opposite* of subtracting $8$ from $x$, which is *adding* $8$ to $x$. Remember to perform the same operation on the right-hand side.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} x-8 &= 6 \\\\ x-8+8 &= 6+8 \\\\ x+0 &= 14 \\\\ x &= 14 \\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535252 17592186045417 false] [17592186054412 1074 "To isolate $x$, we need to perform the *opposite* of subtracting $8$ from $x$, which is *adding* $8$ to $x.$ Remember to perform the same operation on the right-hand side.\n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\n    \t\tx-8 &= 6 \\\n\t\tx-8+8 &= 6+8 \\\n\t\tx+0 &= 14 \\\n\t\tx &= 14\n\t}\n$$" 13194139535252 17592186045417 true] [17592186054413 1071 "What is the solution to the equation $-15 = p - 12$?" 13194139535252 17592186045417 false] [17592186054413 1071 "What is the solution to the equation $-15 = p - 12?$" 13194139535252 17592186045417 true] [17592186054413 1074 "First, let's swap the left and right-hand sides so that the variable is on the left-hand side.\n\n$$\np-12 = -15\n$$\n\nTo isolate $p$, we need to perform the *opposite* of subtracting $12$ from $p$, which is *adding* $12$ to $p$. Remember to perform the same operation on the right-hand side.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} p-12 &= -15 \\\\ p-12 + 12 &= -15 + 12 \\\\ p+0 &=-3 \\\\ p &=-3 \\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535252 17592186045417 false] [17592186054413 1074 "First, let's swap the left and right-hand sides so that the variable is on the left-hand side.\n\n$$\np-12 = -15\n$$\n\nTo isolate $p$, we need to perform the *opposite* of subtracting $12$ from $p$, which is *adding* $12$ to $p.$ Remember to perform the same operation on the right-hand side.\n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\np-12 \t\t&= -15\\\np-12 + 12 \t&= -15 + 12\\\np+0\t\t\t&=-3\\\np\t\t\t&=-3    \t\t\n}\n$$" 13194139535252 17592186045417 true] [17592186054414 1071 "Solve the equation $\\dfrac{1}{2} = \\dfrac{3}{4} + z$." 13194139535252 17592186045417 false] [17592186054414 1071 "Solve the equation $\\dfrac{1}{2} = \\dfrac{3}{4} + z.$" 13194139535252 17592186045417 true] [17592186054414 1074 "First, let's swap the left and right-hand sides so that the variable is on the left-hand side.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{1}{2} &= \\frac{3}{4} + z \\\\\n\\frac{3}{4} + z &= \\frac{1}{2} \\\\\nz + \\frac{3}{4} &= \\frac{1}{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo isolate $z$, we need to perform the *opposite* of adding\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{3}{4}\n$$\n\nto $z$, which is *subtracting*\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{3}{4}\n$$\n\nfrom $z$. Remember to perform the same operation on the right-hand side.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nz + \\frac{3}{4} &= \\frac{1}{2} \\\\\nz + \\frac{3}{4} - \\frac{3}{4} &= \\frac{1}{2} - \\frac{3}{4} \\\\\nz + 0 &= \\frac{2}{4} - \\frac{3}{4} \\\\\nz &= \\frac{2 - 3}{4} \\\\\nz &= -\\frac{1}{4}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535252 17592186045417 false] [17592186054414 1074 "First, let's swap the left and right-hand sides so that the variable is on the left-hand side.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2} & =\\frac{3}{4}+z \\\\ \\frac{3}{4}+z & =\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ z+\\frac{3}{4} & =\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo isolate $z$, we need to perform the *opposite* of adding $\\dfrac{3}{4}$ to $z$, which is *subtracting* $\\dfrac{3}{4}$ from $z.$ Remember to perform the same operation on the right-hand side.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z+\\frac{3}{4} & =\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ z+\\frac{3}{4}-\\frac{3}{4} & =\\frac{1}{2}-\\frac{3}{4} \\\\ z+0 & =\\frac{2}{4}-\\frac{3}{4} \\\\ z & =\\frac{2-3}{4} \\\\ z & =-\\frac{1}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535252 17592186045417 true] [17592186054418 1071 "Solve for $x$, if $-\\dfrac{3}{4}x = 12$." 13194139535252 17592186045417 false] [17592186054418 1071 "Solve for $x,$ if $-\\dfrac{3}{4}x = 12.$" 13194139535252 17592186045417 true] [17592186054418 1074 "First, we multiply both sides by $\\color{blue}4$ to get rid of the fraction:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n-\\frac{3}{4}x &= 12 \\\\\n4 \\cdot (-\\frac{3}{4}x) &= 4 \\cdot 12 \\\\\n4 \\cdot (-\\frac{3}{4}x) &= 48 \\\\\n- 3x &= 48\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we divide both sides of the equation by ${\\color{red}-3}$ to isolate $x$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n-3x &= 48 \\\\\n\\frac{-3x}{-3} &= \\frac{48}{-3} \\\\\n\\frac{-3x}{-3} &= -16 \\\\\nx &= -16\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535252 17592186045417 false] [17592186054418 1074 "First, we multiply both sides by $\\color{blue}4$ to get rid of the fraction:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{3}{4}x & =12 \\\\ 4\\cdot (-\\frac{3}{4}x) & =4\\cdot 12 \\\\ \\cancel{4}\\cdot (-\\frac{3}{\\cancel{4}}x) & =48 \\\\ -3x & =48\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we divide both sides of the equation by ${\\color{red}-3}$ to isolate $x\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-3x & =48 \\\\ \\frac{-3x}{-3} & =\\frac{48}{-3} \\\\ \\frac{\\cancel{-3}x}{\\cancel{-3}} & =-16 \\\\ x & =-16\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535252 17592186045417 true] [17592186054419 1071 "If $-\\dfrac{3}{2}t = 24$, then what is the value of $t$?" 13194139535252 17592186045417 false] [17592186054419 1071 "If $-\\dfrac{3}{2}t = 24$, then what is the value of $t?$" 13194139535252 17592186045417 true] [17592186054419 1074 "We multiply both sides of the equation by the reciprocal of\n\n$$\n-\\dfrac{3}{2}\n$$\n\nwhich is\n\n$$\n\\left(-\\dfrac{3}{2} \\right)^{-1} = -\\dfrac{2}{3}\n$$\n\nThis gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n-\\frac{3}{2}t &= 24 \\\\\n- \\frac{2}{3} \\cdot (-\\frac{3}{2}t) &= -\\frac{2}{3} \\cdot 24 \\\\\n1t &= -\\frac{2}{3} \\cdot 3 \\cdot 8 \\\\\nt &= -16\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535252 17592186045417 false] [17592186054419 1074 "We multiply both sides of the equation by the reciprocal of $-\\dfrac{3}{2},$ which is \n\n$$\n\\left( -\\dfrac{3}{2} \\right)^{-1} = -\\dfrac{2}{3}.\n$$\n\n This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{3}{2}t & =24 \\\\ -\\frac{2}{3}\\cdot (-\\frac{3}{2}t) & =-\\frac{2}{3}\\cdot 24 \\\\ 1t & =-\\frac{2}{\\cancel{3}}\\cdot \\cancel{3}\\cdot 8 \\\\ t & =-16.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535252 17592186045417 true] [17592186054420 1071 "Solve the equation $\\dfrac{3x}{4}-5 = -8$." 13194139535252 17592186045417 false] [17592186054420 1071 "Solve the equation $\\dfrac{3x}{4}-5 = -8.$" 13194139535252 17592186045417 true] [17592186054420 1074 "First, we isolate the fractional term\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{3x}{4}\n$$\n\nby adding $5$ to both sides:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} \\dfrac{3x}{4}-5 &=-8 \\\\[5pt] \\dfrac{3x}{4}-5+5 &=-8+5 \\\\[5pt] \\dfrac{3x}{4}+0 &=-3 \\\\[5pt] \\dfrac{3x}{4} &=-3 \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe fractional coefficient is\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{3}{4}\n$$\n\nso we multiply by its reciprocal\n\n$$\n\\left(\\dfrac{3}{4} \\right)^{-1} = \\dfrac{4}{3}\n$$\n\nThis gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} \\dfrac{3}{4}x &=-3 \\\\[5pt] \\dfrac{4}{3} \\cdot \\dfrac{3}{4}x &= \\dfrac{4}{3} \\cdot (-3) \\\\[5pt] 1 x &=-\\dfrac{4}{\\cancel{3}} \\cdot \\cancel{3} \\\\[5pt] x &= -4. \\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535252 17592186045417 false] [17592186054420 1074 "First, we isolate the fractional term $\\dfrac{3x}{4}$ by adding $5$ to both sides:\n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\n    \t\t\\dfrac{3x}{4}-5 &=-8\\[5pt]\n\\dfrac{3x}{4}-5+5 &=-8+5\\[5pt]\n\\dfrac{3x}{4}+0 &=-3 \\[5pt]\n\\dfrac{3x}{4} &=-3\n\t}\n$$\n\nThe fractional coefficient is $\\dfrac{3}{4},$ so we multiply by its reciprocal $\\left( \\dfrac{3}{4} \\right)^{-1} = \\dfrac{4}{3}.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\n\\dfrac{3}{4}x &=-3 \\[5pt]\n\\dfrac{4}{3} \\cdot \\dfrac{3}{4}x &= \\dfrac{4}{3} \\cdot (-3) \\[5pt]\n1 x &= - \\dfrac{4}{\\cancel{3} } \\cdot \\cancel{3} \\[5pt]\nx &= -4.\n\t}\n$$" 13194139535252 17592186045417 true] [17592186054654 1038 #edb/ref 17592186486391 13194139535252 17592186045417 true] [17592186054654 1038 #edb/ref 17592186486411 13194139535252 17592186045417 true] [17592186054655 1038 #edb/ref 17592186486386 13194139535252 17592186045417 true] [17592186054655 1038 #edb/ref 17592186486417 13194139535252 17592186045417 true] [17592186054656 1038 #edb/ref 17592186486394 13194139535252 17592186045417 true] [17592186054656 1038 #edb/ref 17592186486398 13194139535252 17592186045417 true] [17592186054657 1038 #edb/ref 17592186486396 13194139535252 