begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x}{5}+2 & =\\frac{2}{3} \\\\ \\frac{x}{5}+2-2 & =\\frac{2}{3}-2 \\\\ \\frac{x}{5}+0 & =\\frac{2}{3}-\\frac{6}{3} \\\\ \\frac{x}{5} & =-\\frac{4}{3}\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we apply the multiplication principle, multiplying both sides of the equation by $5:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x}{5} & =-\\frac{4}{3} \\\\ \\cancel{5}\\cdot \\frac{x}{\\cancel{5}} & =5\\cdot (-\\frac{4}{3}) \\\\ x & =-\\frac{20}{3}\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535252 17592186045417 true] [17592186055422 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535252 17592186045417 true] [17592186055422 1071 "If $\\frac{4}{3}x = 8$, then $x =$" 13194139535252 17592186045417 false] [17592186055422 1071 "If $\\frac{4}{3}x=8$, then $x=$" 13194139535252 17592186045417 true] [17592186055422 1074 "First, we multiply both sides of the equation by $3$ to get rid of the fraction:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}\\frac{4}{3}x & =8 \\\\ 3\\cdot \\frac{4}{3}x & =8\\cdot 3 \\\\ \\cancel{3}\\cdot \\frac{4}{\\cancel{3}}x & =8\\cdot 3 \\\\ 4x & =24\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we divide both sides of the equation by $4$ to isolate $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}4x & =24 \\\\ \\frac{4x}{4} & =\\frac{24}{4} \\\\ \\frac{\\cancel{4}x}{\\cancel{4}} & =\\frac{24}{4} \\\\ x & =6\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535252 17592186045417 true] [17592186056012 1064 "To solve an equation like\n\n$$\n2x+4 =10\n$$\n\n, we need to apply both the addition and multiplication principles.\n\nFirst, we apply the addition principle, subtracting $4$ from both sides:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} 2x+4{\\color{red}\\:-\\:4} &=10{\\color{red}\\:-\\:4} \\\\ 2x+0&=6 \\\\ 2x &=6 \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSecond, we apply the multiplication principle, dividing both sides by $2$:\n\n$$\n\\require{cancel} \\begin{aligned} \\dfrac{2x}{\\color{red}2} &=\\dfrac{6}{\\color{red}2}\\ \\dfrac{\\cancel{2}x}{\\cancel{\\color{red}2}} &= 3 \\\\ x &=3 \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo $x=3$ is our solution. Let's plug this back into the original equation, to be sure that it's correct.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n2x + 4 &= 10 \\\\\n2 \\cdot 3 + 4 &= 10 \\\\\n6 + 4 &= 10 \\\\\n10 &= 10✓\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Note:** When solving these types of equations, we always apply the addition principle first, and the multiplication principle second." 13194139535252 17592186045417 false] [17592186056012 1064 "To solve an equation like $2x+4 =10$, we need to apply both the addition and multiplication principles.\n\nFirst, we apply the addition principle, subtracting $4$ from both sides:\n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\n    \t\t2x+4{\\color{red}\\:-\\:4} &=10{\\color{red}\\:-\\:4}\\\n               2x+0&=6\\\n\t\t2x &=6\n\t}\n$$\n\nSecond, we apply the multiplication principle, dividing both sides by $2\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\require{cancel}\n\t\\eqalign{\n    \t\t\\dfrac{2x}{\\color{red}2} &=\\dfrac{6}{\\color{red}2}\\\n                \\dfrac{\\cancel{2}x}{\\cancel{\\color{red}2}} &= 3 \\\n\t\tx &=3\n\t}\n$$\n\nSo $x=3$ is our solution. Let's plug this back into the original equation, to be sure that it's correct.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x+4 & =10 \\\\ 2\\cdot 3+4 & =10 \\\\ 6+4 & =10 \\\\ 10 & =10\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Note:** When solving these types of equations, we always apply the addition principle first, and the multiplication principle second." 13194139535252 17592186045417 true] [17592186056013 1064 "Solving equations with both the addition and multiplication principles can be a lot of work. There are often many steps to write down. However, we don't have to write down all of the steps.\n\nFor example, when using the multiplication principle to solve\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{y}{2} = 5\n$$\n\nthe set of full steps are as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{y}{2} &= 5 \\\\\n2 \\cdot \\frac{y}{2} &= 2 \\cdot 5 \\\\\n\\frac{2y}{2} &= 10 \\\\\n\\frac{2y}{2} &= 10 \\\\\ny &= 10\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHowever, if we cancel out the $2$'s to indicate that they disappear, then we can skip writing down several of the steps.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{y}{2} &= 5 \\\\\n2 \\cdot \\frac{y}{2} &= 2 \\cdot 5 \\\\\ny &= 10\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535252 17592186045417 false] [17592186056013 1064 "Solving equations with both the addition and multiplication principles can be a lot of work. There are often many steps to write down. However, we don't have to write down all of the steps.\n\nFor example, when using the multiplication principle to solve\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{y}{2} = 5,\n$$\n\nthe set of full steps are as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{y}{2} & =5 \\\\ 2\\cdot \\frac{y}{2} & =2\\cdot 5 \\\\ \\frac{2y}{2} & =10 \\\\ \\frac{\\cancel{2}y}{\\cancel{2}} & =10 \\\\ y & =10\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHowever, if we cancel out the $2$'s to indicate that they disappear, then we can skip writing down several of the steps.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{y}{2} & =5 \\\\ \\cancel{2}\\cdot \\frac{y}{\\cancel{2}} & =2\\cdot 5 \\\\ y & =10\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535252 17592186045417 true] [17592186056026 1064 "Solving a linear equation involves **isolating** the variable on one side of the equation.\n\nThis can often be accomplished using the **addition principle**. The addition principle tells us that adding (or subtracting) the same number on both sides of an equation does not change its solution.\n\nFor example, suppose that we want to solve the equation\n\n$$\nx + 4 = 5\n$$\n\nThe variable $x$ is not isolated, because we also have a $+4$ on the left-hand side.\n\nTo isolate $x$, we can perform the *opposite* operation, which is *subtracting* $4$ from $x$. This way, the $+4$ and $-4$ will cancel out to $0$, which will leave $x$ alone. Remember to do this to *both* sides of the equation!\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} x + 4 &= 5 \\\\ x+4\\,{\\color{red}{-}}\\,{\\color{red}{4}} &=5\\,{\\color{red}{-}}\\,{\\color{red}{4}} \\\\ x+(4-4) &=(5-4) \\\\ x + 0 &= 1 \\\\ x &= 1. \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo $x=1$ is our solution. Let's check that our solution is correct by plugging it back into the original equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} x + 4 &= 5 \\\\ 1 + 4&= 5 \\\\ 5 &=5 \\;{\\color{green}{\\checkmark}} \\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535252 17592186045417 false] [17592186056026 1064 "Solving a linear equation involves **isolating** the variable on one side of the equation. \n\nThis can often be accomplished using the **addition principle**. The addition principle tells us that adding (or subtracting) the same number on both sides of an equation does not change its solution.\n\nFor example, suppose that we want to solve the equation\n\n$$\nx + 4 = 5.\n$$\n\nThe variable $x$ is not isolated, because we also have a $+4$ on the left-hand side. \n\nTo isolate $x$, we can perform the *opposite* operation, which is *subtracting* $4$ from $x.$ This way, the $+4$ and $-4$ will cancel out to $0,$ which will leave $x$ alone. Remember to do this to *both* sides of the equation! \n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\n\t\tx + 4 &= 5 \\\n    \t\tx+4\\,{\\color{red}{-}}\\,{\\color{red}{4}} &=5\\,{\\color{red}{-}}\\,{\\color{red}{4}} \\\n\t\tx+(4-4) &=(5-4) \\\n\t\tx + 0 &= 1 \\\n\t\tx &= 1.\n\t}\n$$\n\nSo $x=1$ is our solution. Let's check that our solution is correct by plugging it back into the original equation:\n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\n    \t\tx + 4 &= 5 \\\n\t\t1 + 4&= 5 \\\n\t\t5 &=5 \\;{\\color{green}{\\checkmark}}\n\t}\n$$" 13194139535252 17592186045417 true] [17592186056030 1064 "Suppose that we want to solve the equation\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{2}{3}x = 8\n$$\n\nThis equation has a **fractional coefficient** of\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{2}{3}\n$$\n\nThe term\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{2}{3}x\n$$\n\nmeans that $x$ is being multiplied by $2$ and divided by $3$.\n\nWe can solve this problem by applying the multiplication principle, twice. First, we multiply both sides by ${\\color{blue}{3}}$ to get rid of the fraction:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} \\dfrac{2}{3}x &= 8 \\\\[5pt] {\\color{blue}{3}}\\cdot \\dfrac{2}{3}x &={\\color{blue}{3}}\\cdot 8 \\\\[5pt] \\cancel{\\color{blue}{3}} \\cdot \\dfrac{2}{\\cancel{3}}x &={\\color{blue}{3}}\\cdot 8 \\\\ 2x &=24 \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we divide both sides by ${\\color{red}{2}}$ to isolate $x$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} 2x &=24 \\\\[5pt] \\dfrac{2x}{{\\color{red}{2}}} &=\\dfrac{24}{{\\color{red}{2}}} \\\\[5pt] \\dfrac{\\cancel{2}x}{\\cancel{\\color{red}{2}}} &=\\dfrac{24}{{\\color{red}{2}}} \\\\ x &=12 \\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535252 17592186045417 false] [17592186056030 1064 "Suppose that we want to solve the equation\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{2}{3}x = 8.\n$$\n\nThis equation has a **fractional coefficient** of $\\dfrac{2}{3}.$ The term $\\dfrac{2}{3}x$ means that $x$ is being multiplied by $2$ and divided by $3.$\n\nWe can solve this problem by applying the multiplication principle, twice. First, we multiply both sides by ${\\color{blue}{3}}$ to get rid of the fraction:\n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\n\\dfrac{2}{3}x &= 8 \\[5pt]\n\t\t{\\color{blue}{3}}\\cdot \\dfrac{2}{3}x &={\\color{blue}{3}}\\cdot 8 \\[5pt]\n\\cancel{\\color{blue}{3}} \\cdot \\dfrac{2}{\\cancel{3}}x &={\\color{blue}{3}}\\cdot 8 \\\n\t\t2x &=24\n\t}\n$$\n\nThen, we divide both sides by ${\\color{red}{2}}$ to isolate $x\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\n\t\t2x &=24\\[5pt]\n\t\t\\dfrac{2x}{{\\color{red}{2}}} &=\\dfrac{24}{{\\color{red}{2}}} \\[5pt]\n\\dfrac{\\cancel{2}x}{\\cancel{\\color{red}{2}}} &=\\dfrac{24}{{\\color{red}{2}}} \\\n\t\tx &=12\t\n\t}\n$$" 13194139535252 17592186045417 true] [17592186056031 1064 "When solving equations with fractional coefficients, there is a shortcut! We can complete division and multiplication in a single step by multiplying both sides of the equation by the reciprocal of the fractional coefficient.\n\nFor example, to solve the equation\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{2x}{5}= 6\n$$\n\nwe first express the fraction as a coefficient:\n\n$$\n\\require{cancel} \\begin{aligned} \\dfrac{2}{5}x &= 6 \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe fractional coefficient is\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{2}{5}\n$$\n\n, and its reciprocal is\n\n$$\n\\left(\\dfrac 2 5\\right)^{-1} = {\\color{blue}{\\dfrac 5 2}}\n$$\n\nBecause the product of a fraction and its reciprocal is equal to $1$, we can multiply both sides of the equation by the reciprocal to isolate $x$:\n\n$$\n\\require{cancel} \\begin{aligned} \\dfrac{2}{5}x &= 6 \\\\[5pt] \\underbrace{{\\color{blue}{\\dfrac 5 2}} \\cdot \\dfrac{2}{5}}_{\\large =\\, 1} x &= {\\color{blue}{\\dfrac 5 2}} \\cdot 6 \\\\[5pt] 1 x &= \\dfrac{5\\cdot 6}{2} \\\\[5pt] x &= \\dfrac{30}{2} \\\\[5pt] x &= 15 \\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535252 17592186045417 false] [17592186056031 1064 "When solving equations with fractional coefficients, there is a shortcut! We can complete division and multiplication in a single step by multiplying both sides of the equation by the reciprocal of the fractional coefficient.\n\nFor example, to solve the equation $\\dfrac{2x}{5}= 6,$ we first express the fraction as a coefficient:\n\n$$\n\\require{cancel}\n\t\\eqalign{\n    \t\t\\dfrac{2}{5}x &= 6\n\t}\n$$\n\nThe fractional coefficient is $\\dfrac{2}{5}$, and its reciprocal is $\\left(\\dfrac 2 5\\right)^{-1} = {\\color{blue}{\\dfrac 5 2}}.$ Because the product of a fraction and its reciprocal is equal to $1,$ we can multiply both sides of the equation by the reciprocal to isolate $x\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\require{cancel}\n\t\\eqalign{\n\t\t\\dfrac{2}{5}x &= 6 \\[5pt]\n\t\t\\underbrace{ {\\color{blue}{\\dfrac 5 2}}  \\cdot \\dfrac{2}{5} }_{\\large =\\, 1} x &= {\\color{blue}{\\dfrac 5 2}} \\cdot 6 \\[5pt]   \n\t\t1 x  &= \\dfrac{5\\cdot 6}{2} \\[5pt]   \n\t\tx  &= \\dfrac{30}{2} \\[5pt]   \n\n\t\tx &= 15\n\t}\n$$" 13194139535252 17592186045417 true] [17592186095604 1078 #edb/ref 17592186095609 13194139535252 17592186045417 true] [17592186096548 1071 "Which of the following sequences diverge?\n\n- $a_n = \\dfrac{1}{n^2}$ for $n \\geq 1$\n\n- $a_n  = \\dfrac{n^4}{2n^3+1}$ for $n \\geq 1$\n\n- $a_n = \\dfrac {3 n ^ 5 + 7} {5 n ^ 4 - 3 n ^ 3 }$ for $n\\geq 1?$" 13194139535252 17592186045417 false] [17592186096548 1071 "Which of the following sequences diverge?\n\nI. $a_n = \\dfrac{1}{n^2}$ for $n \\geq 1$\n\nII. $a_n  = \\dfrac{n^4}{2n^3+1}$ for $n \\geq 1$\n\nIII. $a_n = \\dfrac {3 n ^ 5 + 7} {5 n ^ 4 - 3 n ^ 3 }$ for $n\\geq 1?$" 13194139535252 17592186045417 true] [17592186096740 1038 #edb/ref 17592186486388 13194139535252 17592186045417 true] [17592186096740 1038 #edb/ref 17592186486422 13194139535252 17592186045417 true] [17592186096740 1038 #edb/ref 17592186486425 13194139535252 17592186045417 true] [17592186096740 1038 #edb/ref 17592186486456 13194139535252 17592186045417 true] [17592186096741 1038 #edb/ref 17592186486357 13194139535252 17592186045417 true] [17592186096741 1038 #edb/ref 17592186486387 13194139535252 17592186045417 true] [17592186096743 1038 #edb/ref 17592186486359 13194139535252 17592186045417 true] [17592186096743 1038 #edb/ref 17592186486402 13194139535252 17592186045417 true] [17592186096743 1038 #edb/ref 17592186486420 13194139535252 17592186045417 true] [17592186096743 1038 #edb/ref 17592186486441 13194139535252 17592186045417 true] [17592186096743 1038 #edb/ref 17592186486447 13194139535252 17592186045417 true] [17592186096876 1050 420.0 13194139535252 17592186045417 true] [17592186097280 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535252 17592186045417 true] [17592186097280 1071 "What is the limit of the sequence $a_{n} = \\frac{2n + 3}{4n}$ for $n \\ge 1$?" 13194139535252 17592186045417 false] [17592186097280 1071 "What is the limit of the sequence ${a}_{n}=\\frac{2n+3}{4n}$ for $n\\ge 1?$" 13194139535252 17592186045417 true] [17592186097280 1074 "To determine whether  the sequence converges or diverges, we need to determine the limit of the sequence as $n$ approaches infinity:\n\n$$\n{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{a}_{n}={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{2n+3}{4n}\n$$\n\nThe dominant term in the denominator is $4n,$ so we divide the numerator and the denominator by $n$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{2n+3}{4n} & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{(\\frac{2n}{n}-\\frac{3}{n})}{(\\frac{4n}{n})} \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{(2-\\frac{3}{n})}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{2-0}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the limit exists, the sequence converges, and its limit is  $\\frac{1}{2}.$" 13194139535252 17592186045417 true] [17592186109999 1075 #edb/ref 17592186110000 13194139535252 17592186045417 false] [17592186109999 1075 #edb/ref 17592186110001 13194139535252 17592186045417 false] [17592186109999 1075 #edb/ref 17592186110002 13194139535252 17592186045417 false] [17592186109999 1075 #edb/ref 17592186110003 13194139535252 17592186045417 false] [17592186109999 1075 #edb/ref 17592186110004 13194139535252 17592186045417 false] [17592186109999 1075 #edb/ref 17592186485772 13194139535252 17592186045417 true] [17592186109999 1075 #edb/ref 17592186485808 13194139535252 17592186045417 true] [17592186109999 1075 #edb/ref 17592186486039 13194139535252 17592186045417 true] [17592186109999 1075 #edb/ref 17592186486125 13194139535252 17592186045417 true] [17592186109999 1075 #edb/ref 17592186486164 13194139535252 17592186045417 true] [17592186109999 1078 #edb/ref 17592186486039 13194139535252 17592186045417 true] [17592186110506 1074 "First, we rewrite the second factor using the power of quotient rule:\n\n$$\n4^{8} \\cdot (\\frac{7}{4})^{5} = 4^{8} \\cdot \\frac{7^{5}}{4^{5}}\n$$\n\nNext, we rewrite the fractional part of our expression as follows:\n\n$$\n4^{8} \\cdot \\dfrac{7^5}{4^5} = \\dfrac{4^8}{4^5} \\cdot 7^5\n$$\n\nFinally, we simplify using the quotient rule for exponents:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{4^8}{4^5} \\cdot 7^5 =4^{8-5}\\cdot 7^5=4^{3}\\cdot 7^5\n$$" 13194139535252 17592186045417 false] [17592186110506 1074 "First, we rewrite the second factor using the power of quotient rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{4}^{8}\\cdot {(\\frac{7}{4})}^{5}={4}^{8}\\cdot \\frac{{7}^{5}}{{4}^{5}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we rewrite the fractional part of our expression as follows:\n\n$$\n4^{8} \\cdot  \\dfrac{7^5}{4^5} =  \\dfrac{4^8}{4^5} \\cdot 7^5\n$$\n\nFinally, we simplify using the quotient rule for exponents:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{4^8}{4^5} \\cdot 7^5 =4^{8-5}\\cdot 7^5=4^{3}\\cdot 7^5\n$$" 13194139535252 17592186045417 true] [17592186110616 1038 #edb/ref 17592186486366 13194139535252 17592186045417 true] [17592186110616 1038 #edb/ref 17592186486371 13194139535252 17592186045417 true] [17592186110616 1038 #edb/ref 17592186486431 13194139535252 17592186045417 true] [17592186110617 1038 #edb/ref 17592186486461 13194139535252 17592186045417 true] [17592186110618 1038 #edb/ref 17592186486360 13194139535252 17592186045417 true] [17592186110618 1038 #edb/ref 17592186486448 13194139535252 17592186045417 true] [17592186110829 1050 420.0 13194139535252 17592186045417 true] [17592186111379 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535252 17592186045417 true] [17592186111379 1071 "${3}^{4}\\times {({3}^{2})}^{4}=$" 13194139535252 17592186045417 true] [17592186111379 1071 "What is $3^{4} \\cdot (3^{2})^{4}$?" 13194139535252 17592186045417 false] [17592186111379 1074 "First, we rewrite the second factor using the power rule for exponents:\n\n$$\n{({3}^{2})}^{4}={3}^{2\\cdot 4}={3}^{8}\n$$\n\nThen we use the product rule for exponents:\n\n$$\n{3}^{4}\\times {3}^{8}={3}^{4+8}={3}^{12}\n$$" 13194139535252 17592186045417 true] [17592186111884 1064 "Let's recap some of the rules for exponents.\n\n- **Product rule**: to multiply powers of the same base, add the exponents.\n\n- **Quotient rule**: to divide powers of the same base, subtract the exponents.\n\n- **Power rule**: to raise a power to another power, multiply the exponents.\n\n- **Power of product rule**: to raise the product of two numbers to a power, raise each number to that power, and then multiply.\n\n- **Power of quotient rule**: to raise a fraction to a power, raise both the numerator and the denominator to that power.\n\nWe can use a combination of these rules to simplify expressions containing exponents. Let's see an example of this." 13194139535252 17592186045417 true] [17592186111884 1064 "Let's recap some of the rules for exponents.\n\n- **Product rule**: to multiply powers of the same base, add the exponents.\n- **Quotient rule**: to divide powers of the same base, subtract the exponents.\n- **Power rule**: to raise a power to another power, multiply the exponents.\n- **Power of product rule**: to raise the product of two numbers to a power, raise each number to that power, and then multiply.\n- **Power of quotient rule**: to raise a fraction to a power, raise both the numerator and the denominator to that power.\n\nWe can use a combination of these rules to simplify expressions containing exponents. Let's see an example of this." 13194139535252 17592186045417 false] [17592186115059 1071 "Find $A^4$ given that ![](images/45/45b13bcb5bbd8dc54e605acf6d378f582dcb7a4f2ecfff35a7a5343ee30f0515.png)" 13194139535252 17592186045417 false] [17592186115059 1071 "Find $A^4$ given that ![](images/e0/e0e7d5ed9b893352d68931ff5d8fa93f0d0c5173e5955a3f5cdc0cb65d3c3c78.svg)" 13194139535252 17592186045417 true] [17592186115060 1071 "Consider the matrix\n![](images/b0/b070684b11de0098cac091b9132ef6276679177fa6dce1a8129f7951f6013c8c.png)\nFind $f(D)$ if $f(X)=-X^2+3X+2.$" 13194139535252 17592186045417 false] [17592186115060 1071 "Consider the matrix\n![](images/f5/f5b9dd39e03e7a8c984d6a38db8dc32326669d134ff0b5aa012c421308b48351.svg)\nFind $f(D)$ if $f(X)=-X^2+3X+2.$" 13194139535252 17592186045417 true] [17592186115229 1038 #edb/ref 17592186486379 13194139535252 17592186045417 true] [17592186115229 1038 #edb/ref 17592186486381 13194139535252 17592186045417 true] [17592186115229 1038 #edb/ref 17592186486443 13194139535252 17592186045417 true] [17592186115230 1038 #edb/ref 17592186486363 13194139535252 17592186045417 true] [17592186115230 1038 #edb/ref 17592186486453 13194139535252 17592186045417 true] [17592186115471 1050 600.0 13194139535252 17592186045417 true] [17592186116488 1064 "Powers of matrices are similar to powers of numbers. If $A$ is a matrix, then $A^n$ is $A$ multiplied by itself $n$ times: \n\n$$\nA^n = \\underbrace{A\\:\\cdot\\: A\\:\\cdot\\: \\ldots \\:\\cdot\\: A}_{\\Large{n\\text{ times}}}\n$$\n\nFor $A$ to be conformable with itself, $A$ needs to be a square matrix. So we can only take powers of square matrices.\n\nTo demonstrate, suppose that\n![](images/81/81537bed0eafc982114b89fc84baeac44f7a75997c89df5a0f89b8b0a00b2da3.svg)\n\nThen we can calculate $A^2$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{2} & =A\\cdot A \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}3 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3\\cdot 3+(-1)\\cdot 2 & 3\\cdot (-1)+(-1)\\cdot 1 \\\\ 2\\cdot 3+1\\cdot 2 & 2\\cdot (-1)+1\\cdot 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}7 & -4 \\\\ 8 & -1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe say that $A^0 = I,$ the identity matrix of the same dimension as $A.$" 13194139535252 17592186045417 true] [17592186116488 1064 "Powers of matrices are similar to powers of numbers. If $A$ is a matrix, then $A^n$ is $A$ multiplied by itself $n$ times: $$A^n = \\underbrace{A\\:\\cdot\\: A\\:\\cdot\\: \\ldots \\:\\cdot\\: A}_{\\Large{n\\text{ times}}}$$\n\nFor $A$ to be conformable with itself, $A$ needs to be a square matrix. So we can only take powers of square matrices.\n\nTo demonstrate, suppose that $$A=[\\begin{aligned}3 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}].$$\n\nThen we can calculate $A^2$ as follows:\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}{A}^{2} & =A\\cdot A \\\\ & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}3 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\ & =[\\begin{aligned}3\\cdot 3+(-1)\\cdot 2 & 3\\cdot (-1)+(-1)\\cdot 1 \\\\ 2\\cdot 3+1\\cdot 2 & 2\\cdot (-1)+1\\cdot 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\ & =[\\begin{aligned}7 & -4 \\\\ 8 & -1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}$$\n\nWe say that $A^0 = I,$ the identity matrix of the same dimension as $A.$" 13194139535252 17592186045417 false] [17592186116489 1064 "Now that we know how to compute powers of matrices, we can evaluate polynomials where the input is a square matrix.\n\nFor example, consider the polynomial\n\n$$f(X) = X^2 + 4X + 3.$$\n\nNote that, in order to evaluate this polynomial for an input matrix $X,$ we need to interpret any constants as multiples of the identity matrix. So, we interpret the polynomial as\n\n$$f(X) = X^2 + 4X + 3I.$$\n\nFor example, if $A = [\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}],$ then\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}{A}^{2} & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\ & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 4\\end{aligned}],\\end{aligned}$$\n\nand we can evaluate $f(A)$ as follows:\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}f(A) & ={A}^{2}+4A+3I \\\\ & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 4\\end{aligned}]+4[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}]+3[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\ & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 4\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}4 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 8\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{aligned}] \\\\ & =[\\begin{aligned}1+4+3 & 3+4+0 \\\\ 0+0+0 & 4+8+3\\end{aligned}] \\\\ & =[\\begin{aligned}8 & 7 \\\\ 0 & 15\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}$$" 13194139535252 17592186045417 false] [17592186116489 1064 "Now that we know how to compute powers of matrices, we can evaluate polynomials where the input is a square matrix. \n\nFor example, consider the polynomial\n\n$$\nf(X) = X^2 + 4X + 3.\n$$\n\nNote that, in order to evaluate this polynomial for an input matrix $X,$ we need to interpret any constants as multiples of the identity matrix. So, we interpret the polynomial as\n\n$$\nf(X) = X^2 + 4X + 3I.\n$$\n\nFor example, if ![](images/6a/6abb7cbdef9c5d7b23d8519468b174de369cfe4af4b9f8c4578ce7dc74812f63.svg) then\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{2} & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 4\\end{aligned}],\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand we can evaluate $f(A)$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(A) & ={A}^{2}+4A+3I \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 4\\end{aligned}]+4[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}]+3[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 4\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}4 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 8\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1+4+3 & 3+4+0 \\\\ 0+0+0 & 4+8+3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}8 & 7 \\\\ 0 & 15\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535252 17592186045417 true] [17592186117722 1078 #edb/ref 17592186117725 13194139535252 17592186045417 true] [17592186117759 1075 #edb/ref 17592186117760 13194139535252 17592186045417 false] [17592186117759 1075 #edb/ref 17592186117761 13194139535252 17592186045417 false] [17592186117759 1075 #edb/ref 17592186117762 13194139535252 17592186045417 false] [17592186117759 1075 #edb/ref 17592186117763 13194139535252 17592186045417 false] [17592186117759 1075 #edb/ref 17592186117764 13194139535252 17592186045417 false] [17592186117759 1075 #edb/ref 17592186485942 13194139535252 17592186045417 true] [17592186117759 1075 #edb/ref 17592186485943 13194139535252 17592186045417 true] [17592186117759 1075 #edb/ref 17592186486197 13194139535252 17592186045417 true] [17592186117759 1075 #edb/ref 17592186486204 13194139535252 17592186045417 true] [17592186117759 1075 #edb/ref 17592186486214 13194139535252 17592186045417 true] [17592186117759 1078 #edb/ref 17592186485943 13194139535252 17592186045417 true] [17592186117795 1075 #edb/ref 17592186117796 13194139535252 17592186045417 false] [17592186117795 1075 #edb/ref 17592186117797 13194139535252 17592186045417 false] [17592186117795 1075 #edb/ref 17592186117798 13194139535252 17592186045417 false] [17592186117795 1075 #edb/ref 17592186117799 13194139535252 17592186045417 false] [17592186117795 1075 #edb/ref 17592186117800 13194139535252 17592186045417 false] [17592186117795 1075 #edb/ref 17592186485794 13194