 #edb/ref 17592186486376 13194139535252 17592186045417 true] [17592186120014 1050 1020.0 13194139535252 17592186045417 true] [17592186120263 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535252 17592186045417 true] [17592186120263 1071 "![](images/ea/ea1eb590e18a85672f176188f1bab96d85eb1be92d43edd322d1d032500a97ac.png)\n\nConsider the RL circuit shown above. Given that the current $I(t)$ at time $t$ seconds is given by\n\n$$\nI(t)=A{e}^{-14t}+\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t),\n$$\n\nwhere $A,$ $\\alpha ,$ and $\\beta $ are constants, find the value of $\\alpha +\\beta .$" 13194139535252 17592186045417 true] [17592186120263 1071 "![](images/ea/ea1eb590e18a85672f176188f1bab96d85eb1be92d43edd322d1d032500a97ac.png)\n\nConsider the RL circuit shown above. Given that the current $I(t)$ at time $t$ seconds is given by\n$I(t) = Ae^{-14t} + α\\cos (2t) + β\\sin (2t)$,\nwhere $A$, $α$, and $β$ are constants, find the value of $α + β$.\n\n> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question." 13194139535252 17592186045417 false] [17592186120263 1074 "The diagram shows a series RL circuit with a source electromotive force (EMF) of $3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)\\,\\text{V},$ a resistor of resistance $7\\,Ω,$ and an inductor of inductance $\\frac{1}{2}\\,\\text{H}.$\n\nKirchhoff's voltage law states that in a series circuit, the source voltage (EMF) equals the sum of the voltage drops.\n\nIn an RL circuit, there are two voltage drops:\n\n- The voltage drop across the resistor is proportional to the current, with a constant of proportionality $R=7\\,Ω.$\n\n$$\n{V}_{R}=7I\n$$\n\n- The voltage drop across the inductor is proportional to the rate of change of current, with a constant of proportionality $L=\\frac{1}{2}\\,\\text{H}.$\n\n$$\n{V}_{L}=\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\frac{dI}{dt}\n$$\n\nTherefore, by Kirchhoff's voltage law, the differential equation governing the amount of current $I$ in the circuit is\n\n$$\n3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)=7I+\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\frac{dI}{dt}\n$$\n\nwhich gives the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dI}{dt}+14I=6\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t).\n$$\n\nThis is a first-order linear equation with constant coefficients. So, to find the general solution, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\n- To find the complementary solution ${I}_{c},$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{d{I}_{c}}{dt}+14{I}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving using an integration technique, such as separation of variables, we obtain the complementary solution\n\n$$\n{I}_{c}(t)=A{e}^{-14t},\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is a constant of integration.\n\n- Next, we find the particular solution. Note that the right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is sinusoidal with argument $2t.$ So, we assume that the particular solution is also sinusoidal with argument $2t,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{I}_{p}(t)=\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t).\n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha $ and $\\beta ,$ we substitute\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{I}_{p} & =\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t), \\\\ \\frac{d{I}_{p}}{dt} & =-2\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)+2\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\ninto the inhomogeneous equation and group like terms on the left-hand side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(-2\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)+2\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t))+14(\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)) & =6\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t) \\\\ 2(-\\alpha +7\\beta )\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)+2(7\\alpha +\\beta )\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t) & =6\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t) \\\\ (-\\alpha +7\\beta )\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)+(7\\alpha +\\beta )\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t) & =3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}-\\alpha +7\\beta =3\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)) \\\\ 7\\alpha +\\beta =0\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t))\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $\\alpha =-\\frac{3}{50},$ $\\beta =\\frac{21}{50}.$ Therefore, the particular solution is\n\n$$\n{I}_{p}(t)=-\\frac{3}{50}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+\\frac{21}{50}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t).\n$$\n\nNow, since the general solution is given by the sum of the complementary solution and the particular solution, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\nI(t)={I}_{c}(t)+{I}_{p}(t)=A{e}^{-14t}-\\frac{3}{50}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+\\frac{21}{50}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t).\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\alpha =-\\frac{3}{50},\\,\\beta =\\frac{21}{50}\n$$\n\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\alpha +\\beta  & =-\\frac{3}{50}+\\frac{21}{50} \\\\  & =\\frac{18}{50} \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{25}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535252 17592186045417 true] [17592186120270 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535252 17592186045417 true] [17592186120270 1071 "Find a Cartesian equation of the osculating plane for the curve $r(t)$ at the point where $t = 0$, given that\n\n$r(t)|= (\\cos t + t\\sin t)i + (\\sin t - t\\cos t)j + \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}t^{2}k; T(t)|= \\frac{1}{2}(\\cos ti + \\sin tj + \\sqrt{3}k); N(t)|=-\\sin ti + \\cos tj$.\n\nNote that $T$ and $N$ denote the unit tangent and principal normal, respectively." 13194139535252 17592186045417 false] [17592186120270 1071 "Find a Cartesian equation of the osculating plane for the curve $r(t)$ at the point where $t=0,$ given that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\,i+(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)\\,j+\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}{t}^{2}\\,k \\\\ T(t) & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,j+\\sqrt{3}\\,k) \\\\ N(t) & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that $T$ and $N$ denote the unit tangent and principal normal, respectively." 13194139535252 17592186045417 true] [17592186120270 1074 "First, notice that the unit tangent and the principal normal at the point where $t=0$ are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(0) & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡0\\,i+\\operatorname{sin}⁡0\\,j+\\sqrt{3}\\,k)=\\frac{1}{2}\\,i+\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\,k \\\\ N(0) & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡0\\,i+\\operatorname{cos}⁡0\\,j=j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAlso, the position vector of the point where $t=0$ is\n\n$$\np=r(0)=(\\operatorname{cos}⁡0+(0)\\operatorname{sin}⁡0)\\,i+(\\operatorname{sin}⁡0-(0)\\operatorname{cos}⁡0)\\,j+\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}(0)^{2}\\,k=⟨1,0,0⟩.\n$$\n\nNow, we need to find a vector that's perpendicular to the osculating plane. To do that, we use the cross product of the unit tangent $T(0)$ and the principal normal $N(0):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(0)\\times N(0) & =(\\frac{1}{2}\\,i+\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\,k)\\times j \\\\  & =\\left|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ \\frac{1}{2} & 0 & \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2} \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0\\end{aligned}\\right| \\\\  & =\\left|\\begin{aligned}0 & \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2} \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}\\right|\\,i-\\left|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2} & \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2} \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}\\right|\\,j+\\left|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}\\right|\\,k \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\,i+\\frac{1}{2}\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince we are interested only in the direction of the above vector, let's write our perpendicular vector $n$ as \n\n$$\nn=2\\cdot (T(0)\\times N(0))=-\\sqrt{3}\\,i+k=⟨-\\sqrt{3},0,1⟩.\n$$\n\nFinally, let $⟨x,y,z⟩$ denote the position vector of a point on the osculating plane. Since the plane is perpendicular to $n$ and passes through the tip of the position vector $p$, its Cartesian equation can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(⟨x,y,z⟩-p)\\cdot n & =0 \\\\ ⟨x,y,z⟩\\cdot n-p\\cdot n & =0 \\\\ ⟨x,y,z⟩\\cdot n & =p\\cdot n \\\\ ⟨x,y,z⟩\\cdot ⟨-\\sqrt{3},0,1⟩ & =⟨1,0,0⟩\\cdot ⟨-\\sqrt{3},0,1⟩ \\\\ -\\sqrt{3}\\cdot x+0\\cdot y+1\\cdot z & =-\\sqrt{3} \\\\ \\sqrt{3}x-z-\\sqrt{3} & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535252 17592186045417 true] [17592186120276 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535252 17592186045417 true] [17592186120276 1071 "Find the Cartesian equation of the osculating plane for a curve $r(t)$ at the point $P(1, 0, 0)$ where $t = 1$, given that\n\n$T(1) = \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}(i + j), N(1) = k$.\n\nNote that $T$ and $N$ denote the unit tangent and principal normal, respectively." 13194139535252 17592186045417 false] [17592186120276 1071 "Find the Cartesian equation of the osculating plane for a curve $r(t)$ at the point $P(1,0,0)$ where $t=1$, given that\n\n$$\nT(1)=\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}(\\,i+\\,j),\\,N(1)=k.\n$$\n\nNote that $T$ and $N$ denote the unit tangent and principal normal, respectively." 13194139535252 17592186045417 true] [17592186120276 1074 "First, notice that the position vector of the point $P(1,0,0)$ is $p=⟨1,0,0⟩.$\n\nTo find the equation of the osculating plane, we need to find a vector that's perpendicular to it. To do that, we find the cross product of the unit tangent $T(1)$ and the principal normal $N(1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(1)\\times N(1) & =(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}(\\,i+\\,j))\\times k \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\cdot \\left|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}\\right| \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}[\\text{ }\\left|\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}\\right|\\,i-\\left|\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}\\right|\\,j+\\left|\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}\\right|\\,k\\text{ }] \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}(i-j)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince we are interested only in the direction of the above vector, let's write our perpendicular vector $n$ as \n\n$$\nn=\\sqrt{2}\\cdot (T(1)\\times N(1))=i-j=⟨1,-1,0⟩.\n$$\n\nFinally, let $⟨x,y,z⟩$ denote the position vector of a general point on the osculating plane. Since the plane is perpendicular to $n$ and passes through the tip of the position vector $p$, its Cartesian equation can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(⟨x,y,z⟩-p)\\cdot n & =0 \\\\ ⟨x,y,z⟩\\cdot n-p\\cdot n & =0 \\\\ ⟨x,y,z⟩\\cdot n & =p\\cdot n \\\\ ⟨x,y,z⟩\\cdot ⟨1,-1,0⟩ & =⟨1,0,0⟩\\cdot ⟨1,-1,0⟩ \\\\ 1\\cdot x+(-1)\\cdot y+0\\cdot z & =1 \\\\ x-y-1 & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535252 17592186045417 true] [17592186120298 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535252 17592186045417 true] [17592186120298 1071 "Find a parametric equation of the osculating plane for the curve  $r(t)$ at the point where $t=\\pi $, given that\n\n$$\nr(t)=⟨t,\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩,\\,T(\\pi )=\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}⟨1,0,-1⟩,\\,N(\\pi )=⟨0,1,0⟩.\n$$\n\nNote that $T$ and $N$ denote the unit tangent and principal normal, respectively." 13194139535252 17592186045417 true] [17592186120298 1071 "Find a parametric equation of the osculating plane for the curve $r(t)$ at the point where $t = π$, given that\n\n$r(t) = ⟨t, \\cos t, \\sin t⟩, T(π) = \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}⟨1, 0,-1⟩, N(π) = ⟨0, 1, 0⟩$.\n\nNote that $T$ and $N$ denote the unit tangent and principal normal, respectively." 13194139535252 17592186045417 false] [17592186120298 1074 "The osculating plane passes through the point $P$, which is at the tip of the vector $r(\\pi ).$ The vectors $T(\\pi )$ and $N(\\pi )$ are parallel to this plane. Therefore, a parametric equation for the plane can be written as\n\n$$\nR(s,u)=r(\\pi )+s\\,T(\\pi )+u\\,N(\\pi ),\\,s,u\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\n$$\n\n \n\nWe have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(\\pi ) & =⟨\\pi ,\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi ,\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\pi ⟩=⟨\\pi ,-1,0⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, a parametric equation for the osculating plane at the point $(\\pi ,-1,0)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R(s,u) & =r(\\pi )+s\\,T(\\pi )+u\\,N(\\pi ) \\\\  & =⟨\\pi ,-1,0⟩+s\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}⟨1,0,-1⟩+u\\cdot ⟨0,1,0⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}⟨2\\pi +\\sqrt{2}s,\\,-2+2u,\\,-\\sqrt{2}s⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535252 17592186045417 true] [17592186120997 1064 "Consider the curve $C$ parametrized by the vector function\n\n$$\n\\mathbf f (t)= \\big\\langle2 \\sin t, \\: 2\\cos t, \\: t \\big\\rangle.\n$$\n\nAt the point $P$ where $t=\\dfrac{5\\pi}{6},$ the function, unit tangent, and the principal normal are given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(\\frac{5\\pi }{6}) & =\\frac{1}{6}⟨6,\\,-6\\sqrt{3},\\,5\\pi ⟩ \\\\ T(\\frac{5\\pi }{6}) & =\\frac{1}{5}⟨-\\sqrt{15},\\,-\\sqrt{5},\\,\\sqrt{5}⟩ \\\\ N(\\frac{5\\pi }{6}) & =\\frac{1}{2}⟨-1,\\,\\sqrt{3},\\,0⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the unit tangent $\\mathbf T$ and the principal normal $\\mathbf N$ are not collinear, they define a *unique* plane, called the **osculating plane.** In general, the osculating plane to a curve at a point $P$ is the plane of \"greatest contact\" with the curve at $P.$ \n\n![](images/0a/0af5fd8c4fce63bbc15fe35d40e20602fbd1420bcd23f875368ddcb76755cb79.png)\n\nSince the osculating plane contains the unit tangent vector and the principal normal vector, we can write the equation of the osculating plane in parametric form as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R(s,u) & =f(\\frac{5\\pi }{6})+s\\,T(\\frac{5\\pi }{6})+u\\,N(\\frac{5\\pi }{6}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}⟨6,\\,-6\\sqrt{3},\\,5\\pi ⟩+\\frac{s}{5}⟨-\\sqrt{15},\\,-\\sqrt{5},\\,\\sqrt{5}⟩+\\frac{u}{2}⟨-1,\\,\\sqrt{3},\\,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $s, u \\in (-\\infty, \\infty).$\n\nIn general, for a curve $\\mathbf{r}(t),$ the osculating plane at the point $t=t_0$ has the parametric equation\n\n$$\n\\mathbf R(s,u)  = \\mathbf r \\left( t_0 \\right) + s \\, \\mathbf T \\left( t_0 \\right) + u \\, \\mathbf N \\left( t_0 \\right), \\qquad s, u \\in (-\\infty, \\infty),\n$$\n\nwhere $\\mathbf{T}(t_0)$ and $\\mathbf{N}(t_0)$ are the unit tangent and the principal normal to the curve at $t=t_0,$ respectively.\n\n**Note:** A straight line does not have a principal normal, and consequently it does not have an osculating plane either." 13194139535252 17592186045417 true] [17592186120997 1064 "Consider the curve $C$ parametrized by the vector function $$\\mathbf f (t)= \\big\\langle2 \\sin t, \\: 2\\cos t, \\: t \\big\\rangle.$$ At the point $P$ where $t=\\dfrac{5\\pi}{6},$ the function, unit tangent, and the principal normal are given by\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}f(\\frac{5\\pi }{6}) & =\\frac{1}{6}⟨6,\\,-6\\sqrt{3},\\,5\\pi ⟩ \\\\ T(\\frac{5\\pi }{6}) & =\\frac{1}{5}⟨-\\sqrt{15},\\,-\\sqrt{5},\\,\\sqrt{5}⟩ \\\\ N(\\frac{5\\pi }{6}) & =\\frac{1}{2}⟨-1,\\,\\sqrt{3},\\,0⟩.\\end{aligned}$$\n\nSince the unit tangent $\\mathbf T$ and the principal normal $\\mathbf N$ are not collinear, they define a *unique* plane, called the **osculating plane.** In general, the osculating plane to a curve at a point $P$ is the plane of \"greatest contact\" with the curve at $P.$\n\n![](images/0a/0af5fd8c4fce63bbc15fe35d40e20602fbd1420bcd23f875368ddcb76755cb79.png)\n\nSince the osculating plane contains the unit tangent vector and the principal normal vector, we can write the equation of the osculating plane in parametric form as follows:\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}R(s,u) & =f(\\frac{5\\pi }{6})+s\\,T(\\frac{5\\pi }{6})+u\\,N(\\frac{5\\pi }{6}) \\\\ & =\\frac{1}{6}⟨6,\\,-6\\sqrt{3},\\,5\\pi ⟩+\\frac{s}{5}⟨-\\sqrt{15},\\,-\\sqrt{5},\\,\\sqrt{5}⟩+\\frac{u}{2}⟨-1,\\,\\sqrt{3},\\,0⟩\\end{aligned}$$ where $s, u \\in (-\\infty, \\infty).$\n\nIn general, for a curve $\\mathbf{r}(t),$ the osculating plane at the point $t=t_0$ has the parametric equation $$\\mathbf R(s,u) = \\mathbf r \\left( t_0 \\right) + s \\, \\mathbf T \\left( t_0 \\right) + u \\, \\mathbf N \\left( t_0 \\right), \\qquad s, u \\in (-\\infty, \\infty),$$ where $\\mathbf{T}(t_0)$ and $\\mathbf{N}(t_0)$ are the unit tangent and the principal normal to the curve at $t=t_0,$ respectively.\n\n**Note:** A straight line does not have a principal normal, and consequently it does not have an osculating plane either." 13194139535252 17592186045417 false] [17592186120998 1064 "Suppose that a curve $C$ is parametrized by the vector function $\\mathbf f(t).$ Given a point $P$ on the curve with position vector $\\mathbf p,$ we can find the Cartesian equation of the osculating plane at $P$ using the following three steps.\n\n- **Step 1:** First, we calculate the unit tangent $\\mathbf T(t_0)$ and the principal normal $\\mathbf N(t_0).$\n\n- **Step 2:** Next, we calculate the cross product $\\mathbf{n} = \\mathbf T(t_0) \\times \\mathbf N(t_0).$ Recall that $\\mathbf{n}$ will be perpendicular to the plane containing both the tangent $\\mathbf{T}(t_0)$ and normal $\\mathbf{N}(t_0).$\n\n- **Step 3:** Finally, we write down the Cartesian equation of the osculating plane:\n\n$$\n\\big( \\langle x, y, z \\rangle - \\mathbf{p} \\big) \\cdot \\mathbf n = 0\n$$" 13194139535252 17592186045417 true] [17592186120998 1064 "Suppose that a curve $C$ is parametrized by the vector function $\\mathbf f(t).$ Given a point $P$ on the curve with position vector $\\mathbf p,$ we can find the Cartesian equation of the osculating plane at $P$ using the following three steps.\n\n- **Step 1:** First, we calculate the unit tangent $\\mathbf T(t_0)$ and the principal normal $\\mathbf N(t_0).$\n- **Step 2:** Next, we calculate the cross product $\\mathbf{n} = \\mathbf T(t_0) \\times \\mathbf N(t_0).$ Recall that $\\mathbf{n}$ will be perpendicular to the plane containing both the tangent $\\mathbf{T}(t_0)$ and normal $\\mathbf{N}(t_0).$\n- **Step 3:** Finally, we write down the Cartesian equation of the osculating plane: $$\\big( \\langle x, y, z \\rangle - \\mathbf{p} \\big) \\cdot \\mathbf n = 0$$" 13194139535252 17592186045417 false] [17592186124489 1038 #edb/ref 17592186486410 13194139535252 17592186045417 true] [17592186124490 1038 #edb/ref 17592186486458 13194139535252 17592186045417 true] [17592186124496 1038 #edb/ref 17592186486427 13194139535252 17592186045417 true] [17592186124496 1038 #edb/ref 17592186486444 13194139535252 17592186045417 true] [17592186124497 1038 #edb/ref 17592186486397 13194139535252 17592186045417 true] [17592186124535 1038 #edb/ref 17592186486421 13194139535252 17592186045417 true] [17592186124552 1038 #edb/ref