592186045417 true] [17592186152297 1038 #edb/ref 17592186486392 13194139535252 17592186045417 true] [17592186152297 1038 #edb/ref 17592186486423 13194139535252 17592186045417 true] [17592186152558 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535252 17592186045417 true] [17592186152558 1071 "A factory produced $900$ boxes of cereal last year. This year, production is $300%$ greater than last year. How many boxes of cereal did the factory produce this year?\n\nThe factory produced\n{{field-1}}\nboxes of cereal this year." 13194139535252 17592186045417 true] [17592186152558 1071 "A factory produced $900$ boxes of cereal last year. This year, production is $300%$ greater than last year. How many boxes of cereal did the factory produce this year?The factory produced\n{{field-1}}\nboxes of cereal this year." 13194139535252 17592186045417 false] [17592186152558 1074 "To find this year's cereal production, we apply a $300%$ increase to last year's figure.\n\nIncreasing by $300%$ means that we must apply a new percentage of\n\n$$\n100%+300%=400%.\n$$\n\nWe begin by converting the percentage to a decimal:\n\n$$\n400%=\\frac{400}{100}=4\n$$\n\nNow, we multiply the original amount by $4:$\n\n$$\n900\\times 4=3,600\n$$\n\nSo, the factory produced $3600$ boxes of cereal this year." 13194139535252 17592186045417 true] [17592186159620 1078 #edb/ref 17592186159622 13194139535252 17592186045417 true] [17592186159626 1078 #edb/ref 17592186159628 13194139535252 17592186045417 true] [17592186162118 1038 #edb/ref 17592186486383 13194139535252 17592186045417 true] [17592186162120 1038 #edb/ref 17592186486412 13194139535252 17592186045417 true] [17592186162120 1038 #edb/ref 17592186486430 13194139535252 17592186045417 true] [17592186162137 1038 #edb/ref 17592186486446 13194139535252 17592186045417 true] [17592186162137 1038 #edb/ref 17592186486457 13194139535252 17592186045417 true] [17592186162137 1038 #edb/ref 17592186486463 13194139535252 17592186045417 true] [17592186162139 1038 #edb/ref 17592186486393 13194139535252 17592186045417 true] [17592186162139 1038 #edb/ref 17592186486435 13194139535252 17592186045417 true] [17592186162403 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535252 17592186045417 true] [17592186162403 1071 "The joint probability mass function $f(x, y)$ for the discrete random variables $X$ and $Y$ is given in the table below. Find $E[Xe^{Y}]$.\n\n| $f$ | $Y = 0$ | $Y = \\ln 3$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: |\n| $X = 1$ | $0.3$ | $0.1$ |\n| $X = 2$ | $0.5$ | $0.1$ |\n\n> A calculator is required to answer this question." 13194139535252 17592186045417 false] [17592186162403 1071 "The joint probability mass function $f(x,y)$ for the discrete random variables $X$ and $Y$ is given in the table below. Find $\\text{E}[X{e}^{Y}].$\n\n| $f$ | $Y=0$ | $Y=\\operatorname{ln}⁡3$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $X=1$ | $0.3$ | $0.1$ |\n| $X=2$ | $0.5$ | $0.1$ |" 13194139535252 17592186045417 true] [17592186162403 1074 "Suppose $X$ and $Y$ are discrete random variables with supports ${S}_{X}$ and ${S}_{Y},$ respectively, and $f(x,y)$ is their joint probability mass function with support $S={S}_{X}\\times {S}_{Y}.$ If $r:{R}^{2}\\to R$ is a function of two variables, then the rule of the lazy statistician states that\n\n$$\n\\text{E}[r(X,Y)]={\\sum }_{(x,y)\\in S}r(x,y)\\,f(x,y).\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\nr(x,y)=x{e}^{y}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[X{e}^{Y}] & ={\\sum }_{(x,y)\\in S}r(x,y)\\,f(x,y) \\\\  & =(1\\cdot {e}^{0})\\cdot f(1,0)+(1\\cdot {e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3})\\cdot f(1,\\operatorname{ln}⁡3)+(2\\cdot {e}^{0})\\cdot f(2,0)+(2\\cdot {e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3})\\cdot f(2,\\operatorname{ln}⁡3) \\\\  & =1\\cdot f(1,0)+3\\cdot f(1,\\operatorname{ln}⁡3)+2\\cdot f(2,0)+6\\cdot f(2,\\operatorname{ln}⁡3) \\\\  & =1\\cdot 0.3+3\\cdot 0.1+2\\cdot 0.5+6\\cdot 0.1 \\\\  & =0.3+0.3+1+0.6 \\\\  & =2.2.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535252 17592186045417 true] [17592186162404 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535252 17592186045417 true] [17592186162404 1071 "Find $E[XY^{3}]$, given that random variables $X$ and $Y$ have the joint probability density function\n$f(x, y) = {\\frac{2}{9y^{2}}, \\mid 0 \\le x \\le y^{3}, 0 \\le y \\le 3;; 0, \\mid otherwise$." 13194139535252 17592186045417 false] [17592186162404 1071 "Find $\\text{E}[X{Y}^{3}],$ given that random variables $X$ and $Y$ have the joint probability density function\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{9{y}^{2}},\\, & 0\\le x\\le {y}^{3},\\,0\\le y\\le 3; \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535252 17592186045417 true] [17592186162404 1074 "Suppose $X$ and $Y$ are continuous random variables, and $f(x,y)$ is their joint probability density function. If $r:{R}^{2}\\to R$ is a function of two variables, then the rule of the lazy statistician states that\n\n$$\n\\text{E}[r(X,Y)]={∬}_{{R}^{2}}r(x,y)\\,f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\nr(x,y)=x{y}^{3}.\n$$\n\nNow, let's sketch the region $D=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le {y}^{3},\\,0\\le y\\le 3\\},$ where $f(x,y)$ is nonzero.\n\n![](images/68/68da52d856f882614241d72cff4dc3f5947cf16a6c9f58b0aea1c58d7eb375cc.png)\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[X{Y}^{3}] & ={∬}_{{R}^{2}}x{y}^{3}\\,f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}x{y}^{3}\\,f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}{\\int }_{0}^{{y}^{3}}x{y}^{3}\\cdot \\frac{2}{9{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{9}{\\int }_{0}^{3}y{\\int }_{0}^{{y}^{3}}x\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{9}{\\int }_{0}^{3}y{[\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}]}_{x=0}^{x={y}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{9}{\\int }_{0}^{3}{y}^{7}\\text{ }\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{9}{[\\frac{{y}^{8}}{8}]}_{y=0}^{y=3} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{9}(\\frac{{3}^{8}}{8}) \\\\  & =\\frac{729}{8}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535252 17592186045417 true] [17592186166466 1075 #edb/ref 17592186166467 13194139535252 17592186045417 false] [17592186166466 1075 #edb/ref 17592186166468 13194139535252 17592186045417 false] [17592186166466 1075 #edb/ref 17592186166469 13194139535252 17592186045417 false] [17592186166466 1075 #edb/ref 17592186166470 13194139535252 17592186045417 false] [17592186166466 1075 #edb/ref 17592186166471 13194139535252 17592186045417 false] [17592186166466 1075 #edb/ref 17592186485833 13194139535252 17592186045417 true] [17592186166466 1075 #edb/ref 17592186485922 13194139535252 17592186045417 true] [17592186166466 1075 #edb/ref 17592186486094 13194139535252 17592186045417 true] [17592186166466 1075 #edb/ref 17592186486146 13194139535252 17592186045417 true] [17592186166466 1075 #edb/ref 17592186486203 13194139535252 17592186045417 true] [17592186166466 1078 #edb/ref 17592186485833 13194139535252 17592186045417 true] [17592186166835 1038 #edb/ref 17592186486409 13194139535252 17592186045417 true] [17592186166835 1038 #edb/ref 17592186486413 13194139535252 17592186045417 true] [17592186166835 1038 #edb/ref 17592186486436 13194139535252 17592186045417 true] [17592186166835 1038 #edb/ref 17592186486450 13194139535252 17592186045417 true] [17592186166836 1038 #edb/ref 17592186486405 13194139535252 17592186045417 true] [17592186166837 1038 #edb/ref 17592186486428 13194139535252 17592186045417 true] [17592186166837 1038 #edb/ref 17592186486433 13194139535252 17592186045417 true] [17592186166837 1038 #edb/ref 17592186486440 13194139535252 17592186045417 true] [17592186166979 1050 960.0 13194139535252 17592186045417 true] [17592186167543 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535252 17592186045417 true] [17592186167543 1071 "Find the binormal vector to the curve $r(t)$ at the point $t = 0$, given that\n\n$T(0)|= \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}⟨1, 1, 1⟩,; N(0)|= \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}⟨ - 1, 1, 0⟩$.\n\nNote that $T$ and $N$ denote the unit tangent and principal normal, respectively." 13194139535252 17592186045417 false] [17592186167543 1071 "Find the binormal vector to the curve $r(t)$ at the point $t=0,$ given that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(0) & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}⟨1,1,1⟩, \\\\ N(0) & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}⟨-1,1,0⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that $T$ and $N$ denote the unit tangent and principal normal, respectively." 13194139535252 17592186045417 true] [17592186167543 1074 "The binormal vector is given by\n\n$$\nB(t)=T(t)\\times N(t).\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}B(0) & =T(0)\\times N(0) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}⟨1,1,1⟩\\times \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}⟨-1,1,0⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}}\\left|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 1 & 1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 0\\end{aligned}\\right| \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}}[\\text{ }\\left|\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}\\right|\\,i-\\left|\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{aligned}\\right|\\,j+\\left|\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}\\right|\\,k\\text{ }] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}}⟨(0-1),-(0-(-1)),(1-(-1))⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}}⟨-1,-1,2⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535252 17592186045417 true] [17592186168115 1064 "The **binormal vector** to a curve $C$ defined by the vector-valued function $\\mathbf{r}(t)$ is defined as\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{B}(t) = \\mathbf{T}(t) \\times \\mathbf{N}(t),\n$$\n\nwhere $\\mathbf{T}$ and $\\mathbf{N}$ denote the unit tangent and principal normal vectors, respectively.\n\nThe diagram below shows the binormal vector to a curve at some point $P$ on the curve. \n\n![](images/95/95a892eb30b26d1255d29dcaaac552663443c00b2db6fe1e9168a77be061f2c0.png)\n\nSince $\\mathbf B = \\mathbf T\\times \\mathbf N,$ we can conclude that $\\mathbf B$ is perpendicular to both $\\mathbf T$ and $\\mathbf N,$ and it's pointing downward.\n\nAlso, since $\\mathbf T$ and $\\mathbf N$ are perpendicular *unit* vectors, it follows that $\\mathbf B$ is also a unit vector." 13194139535252 17592186045417 true] [17592186168115 1064 "The **binormal vector** to a curve $C$ defined by the vector-valued function $\\mathbf{r}(t)$ is defined as\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{B}(t) = \\mathbf{T}(t) \\times \\mathbf{N}(t),\n$$\n\nwhere $\\mathbf{T}$ and $\\mathbf{N}$ denote the unit tangent and principal normal vectors, respectively.\n\nThe diagram below shows the binormal vector to a curve at some point $P$ on the curve. \n\nTo demonstrate, consider the curve\n\n\\[\n\\mathbf{r}(t) = \\left\\langle 2\\sin t, \\: 2\\cos t, \\: t \\right\\rangle.\n\\]\n\nAt [math]t=\\dfrac{5\\pi}6,[/math] the point on the curve, unit tangent, and principal normal are, respectively, given by\n\n\\begin{align*}\n\\mathbf{r}\\left(\\dfrac{5\\pi}{6}\\right) &= \\left\\langle 1, -\\sqrt3, \\dfrac{5\\pi}{6} \\right\\rangle, \\\\[5pt]\n\n\\mathbf{T}\\left(\\dfrac{5\\pi}{6}\\right) &= \\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt5}\\left\\langle -\\sqrt3, -1, 1 \\right\\rangle, \\\\[5pt]\n\n\\mathbf{N}\\left(\\dfrac{5\\pi}{6}\\right) &= \\dfrac{1}{2}\\left\\langle -1, \\sqrt3, 0 \\right\\rangle.\n\\end{align*}\n\nUsing the formula to calculate the binormal vector, we have\n\n\\[\n\\begin{align}\n\\mathbf{B}\\left(\\dfrac{5\\pi}{6}\\right)\t&= \\mathbf{T}\\left(\\dfrac{5\\pi}{6}\\right) \\times \\mathbf{N}\\left(\\dfrac{5\\pi}{6}\\right) \\\\[3pt]\n\t\t\t\t\t\t\t&= \\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt5}\\left\\langle -\\sqrt3, -1, 1 \\right\\rangle \\times \\dfrac{1}{2}\\left\\langle -1, \\sqrt3, 0 \\right\\rangle \\\\[3pt]\n\t\t\t\t\t\t\t&= \\dfrac{1}{2\\sqrt5}\\, \\begin{array}{|ccc|}\n\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\\mathbf i & \\mathbf j & \\mathbf k \\\\\n\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t-\\sqrt3 & -1 & 1 \\\\\n\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t-1 & \\sqrt3 & 0\n\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\\end{array} \\\\[3pt]\n\t\t\t\t\t\t\t&= \\dfrac{1}{2\\sqrt5}\\left[ \\ \\begin{array}{|cc|}\n\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t-1 & 1 \\\\\n\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\\sqrt3 & 0\n\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\\end{array} \\,\\mathbf i -\n\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\\begin{array}{|cc|}\n\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t-\\sqrt3 & 1 \\\\\n\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t-1 & 0\n\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\\end{array} \\,\\mathbf j +\n\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\\begin{array}{|cc|}\n\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t-\\sqrt3 & -1 \\\\\n\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t-1 & \\sqrt3\n\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\\end{array} \\,\\mathbf k \\ \\right] \\\\[3pt]\n\t\t\t\t\t\t\t&= \\dfrac{1}{2\\sqrt5} \\left\\langle (0-\\sqrt3), -(0-(-1)), (-3-1) \\right\\rangle \\\\[3pt]\n\t\t\t\t\t\t\t&= \\dfrac{1}{2\\sqrt5} \\left\\langle -\\sqrt3, -1, -4 \\right\\rangle.\n\\end{align}\n\\]\n\nBelow is a diagram of the curve [math]\\mathbf{r}(t),[/math] along with its tangent, normal, and binormal vectors at [math]t=\\dfrac{5\\pi}{6}.[/math] Notice that [math]\\mathbf{T}[/math] and [math]\\mathbf{N}[/math] are both orthogonal to [math]\\mathbf{B}.[/math]\n\n![](images/95/95a892eb30b26d1255d29dcaaac552663443c00b2db6fe1e9168a77be061f2c0.png)\n\nSince $\\mathbf B = \\mathbf T\\times \\mathbf N,$ we can conclude that $\\mathbf B$ is perpendicular to both $\\mathbf T$ and $\\mathbf N,$ and it's pointing downward.\n\nAlso, since $\\mathbf T$ and $\\mathbf N$ are perpendicular *unit* vectors, it follows that $\\mathbf B$ is also a unit vector." 13194139535252 17592186045417 false] [17592186198370 1038 #edb/ref 17592186486385 13194139535252 17592186045417 true] [17592186228877 1074 "Let $x$ be the total number of viewers.\n\nWe're told that $40\\%$ of viewers enjoyed documentaries. Thus, the number of viewers who enjoy documentaries is\n$0.4x$.\n\nWe are also told that of those viewers who enjoy documentaries, $50\\%$ preferred nature topics. So, the number of viewers who preferred nature documentaries is\n\n$$\n0.50(0.4x) = 0.20x\n$$\n\nThe expression $0.20x$ represents $20\\%$ of $x$. Therefore, $20\\%$ of all viewers preferred nature documentaries." 13194139535252 17592186045417 false] [17592186228877 1074 "Let $x$ be the total number of viewers.\n\nWe're told that $40\\%$ of viewers enjoyed documentaries. Thus, the number of viewers who enjoy documentaries is \n\n$$\n0.4x.\n$$\n\nWe are also told that of those viewers who enjoy documentaries, $50\\%$ preferred nature topics. So, the number of viewers who preferred nature documentaries is\n\n$$\n0.50(0.4x) = 0.20x.\n$$\n\nThe expression $0.20x$ represents $20\\%$ of $x.$ Therefore, $20\\%$ of all viewers preferred nature documentaries." 13194139535252 17592186045417 true] [17592186228882 1071 "A service adjusted a subscription fee to $90\\%$ of its original cost. A surcharge of $18\\%$ was applied to the adjusted fee. If the subscription originally cost $\\[math]180$, what is the surcharge?" 13194139535252 17592186045417 false] [17592186228882 1071 "A service adjusted a subscription fee to $90\\%$ of its original cost. A surcharge of $18\\%$ was applied to the adjusted fee. If the subscription originally cost $\\[math]180,$ what is the surcharge?" 13194139535252 17592186045417 true] [17592186228882 1074 "First, the adjusted fee is $90\\%$ of the original cost $(\\[math]180){:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n90% \\cdot 180 &= \\frac{90}{100} \\cdot 180 \\\\\n&= 0.9 \\cdot 180 \\\\\n&= 162\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the adjusted fee is $\\[/math]162$. Then, the surcharge is $18\\%$ of this fee:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n18% \\cdot 162 &= \\frac{18}{100} \\cdot 162 \\\\\n&= 0.18 \\cdot 162 \\\\\n&= 29.16\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surcharge is $\\[math]29.16$." 13194139535252 17592186045417 false] [17592186228882 1074 "First, the adjusted fee is $90\\%$ of the original cost $(\\[math]180){:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}90%\\times 180 & =\\frac{90}{100}\\times 180 \\\\  & =0.9\\times 180 \\\\  & =162\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the adjusted fee is $\\[/math]162.$ Then, the surcharge is $18\\%$ of this fee:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}18%\\times 162 & =\\frac{18}{100}\\times 162 \\\\  & =0.18\\times 162 \\\\  & =29.16\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surcharge is $\\[math]29.16.$" 13194139535252 17592186045417 true] [17592186228884 1071 "Find $160\\%$ of $75$, and then $150\\%$ of the result." 13194139535252 17592186045417 false] [17592186228884 1071 "Find $160\\%$ of $75,$ and then $150\\%$ of the result." 13194139535252 17592186045417 true] [17592186228884 1074 "First, we find $160\\%$ of $75{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n160% \\cdot 75 &= \\frac{160}{100} \\cdot 75 \\\\\n&= 1.6 \\cdot 75 \\\\\n&= 120\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we find $150\\%$ of the result $(120){:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n150% \\cdot 120 &= \\frac{150}{100} \\cdot 120 \\\\\n&= 1.5 \\cdot 120 \\\\\n&= 180\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the final answer is $180$." 13194139535252 17592186045417 false] [17592186228884 1074 "First, we find $160\\%$ of $75{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}160%\\times 75 & =\\frac{160}{100}\\times 75 \\\\  & =1.6\\times 75 \\\\  & =120\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we find $150\\%$ of the result $(120){:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}150%\\times 120 & =\\frac{150}{100}\\times 120 \\\\  & =1.5\\times 120 \\\\  & =180\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the final answer is $180.$" 13194139535252 17592186045417 true] [17592186229193 1038 #edb/ref 17592186486434 13194139535252 17592186045417 true] [17592186229193 1038 #edb/ref 17592186486452 13194139535252 17592186045417 true] [17592186229194 1038 #edb/ref 17592186486368 13194139535252 17592186045417 true] [17592186229194 1038 #edb/ref 17592186486404 13194139535252 17592186045417 true] [17592186229195 1038 #edb/ref 17592186486374 13194139535252 17592186045417 true] [17592186229195 1038 #edb/ref 17592186486403 13194139535252 17592186045417 true] [17592186229243 1050 540.0 13194139535252 17592186045417 true] [17592186230167 1064 "In many real-world situations, such as in retail or finance, it is necessary to apply multiple percentages in succession.\n\nTo do so, we perform one percentage calculation and then apply another percentage to the *result* of the previous calculation.\nThis means each new percentage is based on a *new amount*, not on the original number.\n\nTo demonstrate, let's find $40\\%$ of $150$, and then $25\\%$ of the result.\n\nFirst, we find $40\\%$ of $150{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n40% \\cdot 150 &= \\frac{40}{100} \\cdot 150 \\\\\n&= 0.4 \\cdot 150 \\\\\n&= 60\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, after the first step, the result is $60$.\nThen, we find $25\\%$ of the result:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n25% \\cdot 60 &= \\frac{25}{100} \\cdot 60 \\\\\n&= 0.25 \\cdot 60 \\\\\n&= 15\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, after applying both percentages in succession, the final answer is $15$." 13194139535252 17592186045417 false] [17592186230167 1064 "In many real-world situations, such as in retail or finance, it is necessary to apply multiple percentages in succession.\n\nTo do so, we perform one percentage calculation and then apply another percentage to the *result* of the previous calculation.\nThis means each new percentage is based on a *new amount*, not on the original number.\n\nTo demonstrate, let's find $40\\%$ of $150,$ and then $25\\%$ of the result.\n\nFirst, we find $40\\%$ of $150{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}40%\\times 150 & =\\frac{40}{100}\\times 150 \\\\  & =0.4\\times 150 \\\\  & =60\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, after the first step, the result is $60.$\nThen, we find $25\\%$ of the result:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}25%\\times 60 & =\\frac{25}{100}\\times 60 \\\\  & =0.25\\times 60 \\\\  & =15\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, after applying both percentages in succession, the final answer is $15.$" 13194139535252 17592186045417 true] [17592186230171 1064 "When percentages are applied in succession, it is useful to represent their combined effect as a single percentage of the original amount.\n\nThis means that, instead of applying one percentage and then another, we can multiply their decimal multipliers to find an overall multiplier (or percentage) for the total change.\n\nFor example, suppose that a city’s library membership rose to $150\\%$ of the previous year’s total. Of these members, $40\\%$ borrowed digital books. What percentage of last year’s members borrowed digital books?\n\nFirst, let $x$ be the number of members in the previous year.\n\nWe're told that membership rose to $150\\%$ of the previous year’s total. Thus, the new number of members is\n\n$$\n1.5x\n$$\n\nWe are also told that of those members, $40\\%$ borrowed digital books. So, the number of members who borrowed digital books is\n\n$$\n0.4(1.5x) = 0.6x\n$$\n\nThe expression $0.6x$ represents $60\\%$ of $x$. Therefore, $60\\%$ of last year’s members borrowed digital books." 13194139535252 17592186045417 false] [17592186230171 1064 "When percentages are applied in succession, it is useful to represent their combined effect as a single percentage of the original amount. \n\nThis means that, instead of applying one percentage and then another, we can multiply their decimal multipliers to find an overall multiplier (or percentage) for the total change. \n\nFor example, suppose that a city’s library membership rose to $150\\%$ of the previous year’s total. Of these members, $40\\%$ borrowed digital books. What percentage of last year’s members borrowed digital books?\n\nFirst, let $x$ be the number of members in the previous year.\n\nWe're told that membership rose to $150\\%$ of the previous year’s total. Thus, the new number of members is \n\n$$\n1.5x.\n$$\n\nWe are also told that of those members, $40\\%$ borrowed digital books. So, the number of members who borrowed digital books is\n\n$$\n0.4(1.5x) = 0.6x.\n$$\n\nThe expression $0.6x$ represents $60\\%$ of $x.$ Therefore, $60\\%$ of last year’s members borrowed digital books." 13194139535252 17592186045417 true] [17592186295080 1071 "Consider the matrix $A=[\\begin{aligned}0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}].$ If $f(X)={X}^{2}+1$, find $f(A).$\n\n<table align=\"l\" cellpadding=\"0\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"30px\" rowspan=\"2\">$f(A)=\\,\\,$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"30px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">\n{{field-1}}\n</td>\n<td align=\"center\">\n{{field-2}}\n</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">\n{{field-3}}\n</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"30px\">\n{{field-4}}\n</td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>" 13194139535252 17592186045417 true] [17592186295080 1071 "Consider the matrix $A=[\\begin{aligned}0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}].$ If $f(X)={X}^{2}+1$, find $f(A).$\n\n| $f(A)=\\,\\,$ |  | {{field-1}} | {{field-2}} |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  | {{field-3}} | {{field-4}} |  |  |" 13194139535252 17592186045417 false] [17592186310974 1075 #edb/ref 17592186485818 13194139535252 17592186045417 true] [17592186310974 1078 #edb/ref 17592186310228 13194139535252 17592186045417 false] [17592186310974 1078 #edb/ref 17592186485818 13194139535252 17592186045417 true] [17592186311743 1074 "The diagram shows a series RL circuit with a source electromotive force (EMF) of $3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t)\\,\\text{V},$ a resistor of resistance $6\\,Ω,$ and an inductor of inductance $\\frac{1}{2}\\,\\text{H}.$\n\nKirchhoff's voltage law states that in a series circuit, the source voltage (EMF) equals the sum of the voltage drops.\n\nIn an RL circuit, there are two voltage drops:\n\n- The voltage drop across the resistor is proportional to the current, with a constant of proportionality $R=6\\,Ω.$\n\n$$\n{V}_{R}=6I\n$$\n\n- The voltage drop across the inductor is proportional to the rate of change of current, with a constant of proportionality $L=\\frac{1}{2}\\,\\text{H}.$\n\n$$\n{V}_{L}=\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\frac{dI}{dt}\n$$\n\nTherefore, by Kirchhoff's voltage law, the differential equation governing the amount of current $I$ in the circuit is\n\n$$\n3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t)=6I+\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\frac{dI}{dt}\n$$\n\nwhich gives the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dI}{dt}+12I=6\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t).\n$$\n\nThis is a first-order linear equation with constant coefficients. So, to find the general solution, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\n- To find the complementary solution ${I}_{c},$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{d{I}_{c}}{dt}+12{I}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving using an integration technique, such as separation of variables, we obtain the complementary solution\n\n$$\n{I}_{c}(t)=A{e}^{-12t},\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is a constant of integration.\n\n- Next, we find the particular solution. Note that the right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is sinusoidal with argument $5t.$ So, we assume that the particular solution is also sinusoidal with argument $5t,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{I}_{p}(t)=\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t)+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t).\n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha $ and $\\beta ,$ we substitute\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{I}_{p} & =\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t)+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t), \\\\ \\frac{d{I}_{p}}{dt} & =-5\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t)+5\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\ninto the inhomogeneous equation and group like terms on the left-hand side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(-5\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t)+5\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t))+12(\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t)+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t)) & =6\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t) \\\\ (-5\\alpha +12\\beta )\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t)+(12\\alpha +5\\beta )\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t) & =6\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}-5\\alpha +12\\beta =6\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t)) \\\\ 12\\alpha +5\\beta =0\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t))\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $\\alpha =-\\frac{30}{169},$ $\\beta =\\frac{72}{169}.$ Therefore, the particular solution is\n\n$$\n{I}_{p}(t)=-\\frac{30}{169}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t)+\\frac{72}{169}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t).\n$$\n\nNow, since the general solution is given by the sum of the complementary solution and the particular solution, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\nI(t)={I}_{c}(t)+{I}_{p}(t)=A{e}^{-12t}-\\frac{30}{169}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t)+\\frac{72}{169}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t).\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\alpha =-\\frac{30}{169},\\,\\beta =\\frac{72}{169}\n$$\n\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\alpha +\\beta  & =-\\frac{30}{169}+\\frac{72}{169} \\\\  & =\\frac{42}{169}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535252 17592186045417 true] [17592186311743 1074 "The diagram shows a series RL circuit with a source electromotive force (EMF) of $3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t)\\,\\text{V},$ a resistor of resistance $6\\,Ω,$ and an inductor of inductance $\\frac{1}{2}\\,\\text{H}.$\n\nKirchhoff's voltage law states that in a series circuit, the source voltage (EMF) equals the sum of the voltage drops.\n\nIn an RL circuit, there are two voltage drops:\n\n- The voltage drop across the resistor is proportional to the current, with a constant of proportionality $R=6\\,Ω.$\n\n$$\n{V}_{R}=6I\n$$\n\n- The voltage drop across the inductor is proportional to the rate of change of current, with a constant of proportionality $L=\\frac{1}{2}\\,\\text{H}.$\n\n$$\n{V}_{L}=\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\frac{dI}{dt}\n$$\n\nTherefore, by Kirchhoff's voltage law, the differential equation governing the amount of current $I$ in the circuit is\n\n$$\n3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t)=6I+\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\frac{dI}{dt}\n$$\n\nwhich gives the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dI}{dt}+12I=6\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t).\n$$\n\nThis is a first-order linear equation with constant coefficients. So, to find the general solution, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\n- To find the complementary solution ${I}_{c},$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{d{I}_{c}}{dt}+12{I}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving using an integration technique, such as separation of variables, we obtain the complementary solution\n\n$$\n{I}_{c}(t)=A{e}^{-12t},\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is a constant of integration.\n\n- Next, we find the particular solution. Note that the right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is sinusoidal with argument $5t.$ So, we assume that the particular solution is also sinusoidal w